# readline -- the line editor SpiderMonkey's shell and gjs's REPL are built
# against, and the first of the four packages in the JavaScript chain.
#
# WHY IT IS PINNED HERE AND NOT IN versions.env, EVEN THOUGH IT IS AN LFS
# PACKAGE. versions.env is generated ("do not edit") by tools/pin-versions.sh
# from a fixed PACKAGES array, and it is rewritten WHOLESALE on every run --
# pinning a subset is not an option the script offers. So there were two ways to
# put readline there, and both are worse than this:
#
#   * hand-adding READLINE_* to versions.env contradicts the file's own header
#     and would be destroyed by the next `make pin`, silently, leaving this
#     recipe pointing at variables that no longer exist;
#   * adding readline to the PACKAGES array is the correct long-term home, but
#     it only takes effect through a full regeneration that re-downloads and
#     re-hashes all 39 LFS tarballs -- a change to every pin in the tree, landed
#     in a pull request about a JavaScript engine.
#
# README's "Versions" section already names openssl, cmake, ninja, go and rust
# as pinning in their own pkg.env for the same shape of reason. This joins them.
# The version is still LFS 12.4's (8.3), so nothing here disagrees with the book.
SRC_URL=https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/readline/readline-8.3.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=fe5383204467828cd495ee8d1d3c037a7eba1389c22bc6a041f627976f9061cc
SRC_DIR=readline-8.3

# --with-curses is not a preference, it is what makes readline.pc correct.
# Without it readline decides the termcap functions live in a separate -ltermcap
# and writes that into its .pc file; nothing here ships a termcap library, so
# every later pkg-config consumer would be handed a link line naming a library
# that does not exist. ncurses provides those functions and is already packaged.
#
# --disable-static: nothing in this distribution links readline statically, and
# the archive is half the installed size.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static --with-curses"

# SHLIB_LIBS="-lncursesw" is the line that makes this package usable, and its
# absence is silent in exactly the way this repository keeps getting caught by.
#
# Without it libreadline.so is linked with NO reference to ncurses at all: it
# builds, it installs, and every symbol it needs from the terminal library is
# left undefined for whoever links it later. Both consumers in this chain then
# fail in a way that names neither readline nor ncurses --
#
#   * SpiderMonkey's --enable-readline finds the header and the library and
#     dies at link time on tgetent/tputs;
#   * gjs's meson COMPILES AND RUNS a readline("foo") probe (see its pkg.env),
#     and when the link fails it does not stop -- with -Dreadline=auto it
#     quietly builds a gjs with no interactive shell.
#
# So the flag is passed to the install as well as to the build. The install
# relinks, and a target that relinks without it would undo the whole point.
MAKE_ARGS="SHLIB_LIBS=-lncursesw"
MAKE_INSTALL_ARGS="SHLIB_LIBS=-lncursesw install"
